## Choosing Fedora

### Initial thought process

The main reason I left Omarchy was the 1 TB SSD being used exclusively by
Omarchy, as covered in [why-i-left.md](why-i-left.md). I wanted both Windows
and Omarchy, or whatever replaced it, to share that drive. Although Omarchy
was overwhelming at times, I knew more use would have made me more
comfortable with it eventually. But I wanted to start over with something
simpler, with room to customize later once I got used to Linux's default
functions.

So I started searching for distros with those qualities, along with solid
NVIDIA driver support. I had a few in mind: Ubuntu and Kali, which I'd been
introduced to at university; Fedora, my original pick before Omarchy caught
my attention; and Arch Linux.

### Distro pick

**Ubuntu** is simple for beginners and the most popular distro, but I'd
already used it through my course. I wanted something newer to me, that I
could build on my own.

**Kali** is excellent for pentesting, but beyond that I don't think it's a
good OS to learn Linux on day to day. For pentesting itself, running it in a
VM, so my actual computer wouldn't be at risk, is the better choice anyway.
So I ruled it out too.

**Arch Linux**, after more research, turned out to be genuinely complex to
install and isn't recommended for beginners. I took it off the list quickly.

**Fedora** was my original pick for dual booting before Omarchy took my
attention. Researching it again, I found the UI clean and customizable,
exactly what I was looking for. It also had support for NVIDIA drivers,
although proprietary rather than open source. Fedora's choice between KDE
Plasma and GNOME stood out to me too, and I went with Plasma since I found
it more customizable than GNOME. Fedora became my choice.
